```css
/* Mobile-first base: stacked */
.container { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
.child { width: 100%; }

/* Tablet: 2 columns */
@media (min-width: 601px) {
  .container { flex-direction: row; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .child:first-child { width: 100%; }
  .child:nth-child(2), .child:nth-child(3) { width: 50%; }
}

/* Desktop: 3 columns */
@media (min-width: 1025px) {
  .child { width: 33.33%; }
}
```
A wrong answer using `max-width` queries in descending order (desktop-first) reveals the student hasn't internalized mobile-first thinking — they'll likely override styles unnecessarily and create specificity conflicts. Missing `flex-wrap: wrap` shows they don't understand flexbox wrapping behavior.

